    "content": "1  /*******************************************************************************\n     2\n     3                         Transformation pipeline manager\n     4\n     5                      Thomas Knudsen, 2016-05-20/2016-11-20\n     6\n     7  ********************************************************************************\n     8\n     9      Geodetic transformations are typically organized in a number of\n    10      steps. For example, a datum shift could be carried out through\n    11      these steps:\n    12\n    13      1. Convert (latitude, longitude, ellipsoidal height) to\n    14         3D geocentric cartesian coordinates (X, Y, Z)\n    15      2. Transform the (X, Y, Z) coordinates to the new datum, using a\n    16         7 parameter Helmert transformation.\n    17      3. Convert (X, Y, Z) back to (latitude, longitude, ellipsoidal height)\n    18\n    19      If the height system used is orthometric, rather than ellipsoidal,\n    20      another step is needed at each end of the process:\n    21\n    22      1. Add the local geoid undulation (N) to the orthometric height\n    23         to obtain the ellipsoidal (i.e. geometric) height.\n    24      2. Convert (latitude, longitude, ellipsoidal height) to\n    25         3D geocentric cartesian coordinates (X, Y, Z)\n    26      3. Transform the (X, Y, Z) coordinates to the new datum, using a\n    27         7 parameter Helmert transformation.\n    28      4. Convert (X, Y, Z) back to (latitude, longitude, ellipsoidal height)\n    29      5. Subtract the local geoid undulation (N) from the ellipsoidal height\n    30         to obtain the orthometric height.\n    31\n    32      Additional steps can be added for e.g. change of vertical datum, so the\n    33      list can grow fairly long. None of the steps are, however, particularly\n    34      complex, and data flow is strictly from top to bottom.\n    35\n    36      Hence, in principle, the first example above could be implemented using\n    37      Unix pipelines:\n    38\n    39      cat my_coordinates | geographic_to_xyz | helmert | xyz_to_geographic > my_transformed_coordinates\n    40\n    41      in the grand tradition of Software Tools [1].\n    42\n    43      The proj pipeline driver implements a similar concept: Stringing together\n    44      a number of steps, feeding the output of one step to the input of the next.\n    45\n    46      It is a very powerful concept, that increases the range of relevance of the\n    47      proj.4 system substantially. It is, however, not a particularly intrusive\n    48      addition to the code base: The implementation is by and large completed by\n    49      adding an extra projection called \"pipeline\" (i.e. this file), which\n    50      handles all business.\n    51\n    52      Syntactically, the pipeline system introduces the \"+step\" keyword (which\n    53      indicates the start of each transformation step), the \"+omit_fwd\" and\n    54      \"+omit_inv\" keywords (which indicate that a given transformation step\n    55      should be omitted when the pipeline is executed in forward, resp. inverse\n    56      direction), and reintroduces the +inv keyword (indicating that a given\n    57      transformation step should run in reverse, i.e. forward, when the pipeline\n    58      is executed in inverse direction, and vice versa).\n    59\n    60      Hence, the first transformation example above, can be implemented as:\n    61\n    62      +proj=pipeline +step proj=cart +step proj=helmert <ARGS> +step proj=cart +inv\n    63\n    64      Where <ARGS> indicate the Helmert arguments: 3 translations (+x=..., +y=...,\n    65      +z=...), 3 rotations (+rx=..., +ry=..., +rz=...) and a scale factor (+s=...).\n    66      Following geodetic conventions, the rotations are given in Milliarcseconds,\n    67      and the scale factor is given as parts-per-million.\n    68\n    69      [1] B. W. Kernighan & P. J. Plauger: Software tools. Addison-Wesley, 1976, 338 pp.\n    70\n    71  ********************************************************************************\n    72\n    73  Thomas Knudsen, thokn@sdfe.dk, 2016-05-20\n    74\n    75  ********************************************************************************\n    76  * Copyright (c) 2016, Thomas Knudsen / SDFE\n    77  *\n    78  * Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a\n    79  * copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"),\n    80  * to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation\n    81  * the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense,\n    82  * and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the\n    83  * Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n    84  *\n    85  * The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included\n    86  * in all copies or substantial portions of the Software.\n    87  *\n    88  * THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS\n    89  * OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\n    90  * FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL\n    91  * THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\n    92  * LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING\n    93  * FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER\n    94  * DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.\n    95  *\n    96  ********************************************************************************/\n    97\n    98  #define PJ_LIB__\n    99  #include \"proj_internal.h\"\n   100  #include <projects.h>\n   101\n   102  #include <assert.h>\n   103  #include <stddef.h>\n   104  #include <errno.h>\n   105  PROJ_HEAD(pipeline,         \"Transformation pipeline manager\");\n   106\n   107  /* Projection specific elements for the PJ object */\n 